title: Sorting and postings
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: A sort either fetches every match's key or walks an index until the page is full, by estimated cost; frequent values get a posting.

The filter yields a bitmap first, so the number of matches is known ''exactly and for
free'' before sorting. The sort then either:

* ''fetches'' the sort key of each match and sorts them — about one random read per match, weighted four walk steps each (`FETCH_COST`); or
* ''walks'' the ordered postings — or the forest, for a path sort — testing each id against the match bitmap until the page is full: about page × holders / matches steps, if the matches are spread along the index.

It takes the cheaper by that estimate. No statistics are kept: the match count is
exact, and the holders are the field's presence count (or the walk's subtree). The
count alone is not enough: a 2 500-file folder is "large", yet walking the date index
of a 5-million-file repository for a hundred of its files reads some 200 000 keys. A
path walk starts at the lowest node whose descendants hold every match, not at the
forest's root, and does not go down into a node whose descendants hold none: a node
with no child costs no read, a folder its descendant bitmap. The estimate assumes the
matches spread along the index, and "the files not yet classified" — when they are
classified in path order — all sit at its end; a walk that read every node before
them read three keys each, every one at a random place of the store (140 000 keys,
minutes on a cold disk, for one result). It now reads the folders it meets. What is
left is one flat folder of many non-matching files before the first match. Keyset pagination is the same for both: the cursor carries the last
sort key and uuid, and each strategy resumes strictly after it.

!! A record of several values is met several times

A record sorts on its smallest value ascending, its largest descending — its
''representative''. Its postings list it once per value, so a walk meets it
again at each of the others. Telling the first meeting from the next ones used to
read the record's values: one read per entry walked, and on a field where every
record holds several values — tags — one read per holder of a frequent value, until
the walk ran over its budget and the page fell back to fetching every match
(26 848 keys for a page of 50 over 16 000 records of three tags each).

Nothing is read any more. The pages follow one another from the first, so a walk
meets a record ''first'' at its representative: a record met that was not given yet
is at its place, and one already given is a repeat. The cursor therefore carries the
ids given so far that hold several values of the sort field (the field's `multi`
set; a record of one value is listed once and needs no memory), and the walk skips
them — a posted value's holders are taken whole, minus those. Either strategy adds
its page's ids to the cursor, so a walked page may follow a fetched one. The cursor
stays with the client: nothing is resident in the daemon between two requests
([[How queries are evaluated]]). A text too long for its key to hold whole is still
read, to order the texts sharing its first bytes.

`tests/kv_cost.rs`: a page of 50 sorted on such a field reads the same number of keys
on 2 000 records and on 16 000. `tests/index_page_strategies.rs` holds pages produced
by alternating strategies to the oracle's.

!! Postings

Every value is a ''posting'' — its holders as one set, a key per 65 536 ids — written in
the transaction of the row, and kept, exact, until its last holder goes. (Until November
2026 only a value reaching 64 holders was; a rarer one lived in a ''value partition'', a
key per holder, and every reader had both cases. A record is now in a value's posting while
one of its rows has the key, so taking a row off reads the field's rows once.) An equality
reads the posting; a range or a `!=` takes the postings of the values in range whole, so
`rating > 4` reads five postings, not half the repository. The cost of such a walk is
the keys it reads — a key per chunk of each value in the range —, which the histogram
counts per bucket beside its rows, so a field of ten values and one of a million are told
apart without reading a posting. A
sorted walk hands a posted value's holders over as one run, and a page sorted on a
field of ten values costs about the page over the density of its ties, however large
the repository.

A range over a numeric or date field with many distinct values (`mfr_size > 1 MB`)
reads its rows inside the range, contiguously, or probes its candidates when a narrower
operand gives few ([[Query planner]]). A ''bit-sliced index'', a bitmap per bit of the
value, would answer it in a fixed number of bitmaps however wide the range — and rewrite
up to 64 of them on each value change. It was ''dropped'' (user, November 2026): cold on
100 000 files, a range holding 12 % of them reads 11 786 keys in about 100 ms.
